{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=}}
'''Taranaki-King Country''' is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. The current MP for Taranaki-King Country is [[Barbara Kuriger]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]]. She has held this position since the 2014 general election.

==Population centres==
Taranaki-King Country stretches down the western coast of the North Island, starting at the outskirts of [[Hamilton, New Zealand|Hamilton]], through to the [[King Country]] towns of [[Te Awamutu]], [[Ōtorohanga]] and [[Te Kūiti]], and ending in the northern [[Taranaki]] region, to take in the eastern and southern section of the [[New Plymouth District]], including the communities of [[Waitara, New Zealand|Waitara]], [[Inglewood, New Zealand|Inglewood]], [[Egmont Village]] and [[Midhirst]], and all of [[Stratford District, New Zealand|Stratford District]] except [[Stratford, New Zealand|Stratford]] itself. From {{NZ election link year|2008}}, it has included the town of [[Raglan, New Zealand|Raglan]]. The 2025 boundary review saw the electorate lose its part of [[Waitara, New Zealand|Waitara]] to [[New Plymouth (New Zealand electorate)|New Plymouth]] and gain [[Taumarunui]] from [[Rangitīkei (New Zealand electorate)|Rangitīkei]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=11 September 2025 |page=8 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Taranaki-King Country.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The boundaries have gradually been expanded as the population has fallen, relative to the overall population of the country. At the 2013 revision the proposed boundaries received the third highest number (25) of objections in the country.<ref>[http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/objections_to_proposed_electoral_districts_2014_revised_17_january.pdf Objections to Proposed Electoral Districts]</ref> After the 2013 revision the constituency covered parts of 3 regional councils and 7 district councils ([[Waikato District]], [[Waipa District]], [[Ōtorohanga District]], [[Waitomo District]], [[New Plymouth District]], [[Stratford District, New Zealand|Stratford District]], [[Ruapehu District]]), including [[Hamilton Airport (New Zealand)|Hamilton Airport]].

==History==
The seat was created ahead of the introduction of [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] voting in {{NZ election link year|1996}} from most of the old {{NZ electorate link|King Country}} seat with parts of {{NZ electorate link|Taranaki}}, {{NZ electorate link|Waitotara}} in the south and {{NZ electorate link|Waipa}} in the area around Hamilton. All these seats were safe National seats covering rural areas traditionally loyal to the National Party, the new seat remained faithful to old allegiances in the face of a large swing to [[New Zealand First]] in the central North Island at the {{NZ election link|1996}}. The first MP for Taranaki-King Country was the then [[Prime Minister of New Zealand]], [[Jim Bolger]].

Having been ousted from the leadership of his party, Bolger accepted the role of [[List of ambassadors of New Zealand to the United States|Ambassador to the United States]] in the middle of 1998, and triggered the {{By-election link|Taranaki-King Country|1998}}. Despite a large swing to [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]] candidate [[Owen Jennings]], Bolger's chosen successor [[Shane Ardern]] won a narrow victory on a heavily reduced turnout. Since the 1998 by-election, Taranaki-King Country has reverted to form, giving Ardern two out of every three votes cast in {{NZ election link year|2005}}, {{NZ election link year|2008}} and {{NZ election link year|2011}}.

